Let me look at the `write_encoded_tm_str` function more carefully. The issue is a stack-buffer-overflow READ 4. Let me look at the `codecvt_result` usage. The `write_codecvt` function uses `f.in` with `std::begin(out.buf)` to `std::end(out.buf)`. If the input string is longer than 32 code units, `f.in` would return `partial`. But the issue is a READ.

Let me look at the `write_encoded_tm_str` function again - specifically the surrogate pair handling:

```cpp
if (sizeof(code_unit) == 2 && c >= 0xd800 && c <= 0xdfff) {
    // surrogate pair
    ++p;
    if (p == unit.end || (c & 0xfc00) != 0xd800 ||
        (*p & 0xfc00) != 0xdc00) {
```

This reads `*p` after `++p`. If `p` was at `unit.end - 1`, then `++p` makes `p == unit.end`, and the check `p == unit.end` should catch it. But wait, the check is `p == unit.end || ...` - if `p == unit.end` is true, the `||` short-circuits and `*p` is not evaluated. So that's fine.

Let me look at the `write_tm_str` for non-char types:

```cpp
template <typename Char, typename OutputIt,
          FMT_ENABLE_IF(!std::is_same<Char, char>::value)>
auto write_tm_str(OutputIt out, string_view sv, const std::locale& loc)
    -> OutputIt {
  codecvt_result<Char> unit;
  write_codecvt(unit, sv, loc);
  return copy_str<Char>(unit.buf, unit.end, out);
}
```

This is for `Char != char`. But in the fuzzer, `Char` is `char` since we're using `fmt::format_to` with a `memory_buffer` of char. So `write_tm_str` with `Char=char` calls `write_encoded_tm_str`.

Let me look at the `write_encoded_tm_str` more carefully. The `codecvt_result` has `max_size = 32`. The `f.in` call:

```cpp
auto result = f.in(mb, in_buf.begin(), in_buf.end(), from_next,
                   std::begin(out.buf), std::end(out.buf), out.end);
```

If the input is longer than 32 code units, `f.in` returns `partial` and throws. But what if the input is exactly at the boundary? Let me think about the READ 4 issue.

